12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain String Immutability and String Operations \(+, *, in, not in\). Why are strings designed to be immutable in Python, and how does this affect memory efficiency and security?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Concept of String Immutability:) Tj
T*
(   Once a string object is created in Python heap memory, its characters and size cannot be) Tj
T*
(   modified in-place. Attempting an item assignment such as s[0] = 'X' raises a TypeError: 'str') Tj
T*
(   object does not support item assignment.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Rationales for String Immutability:) Tj
T*
(   - Hashability & Dictionary Keys: Because strings are immutable, their cryptographic/hash value) Tj
T*
(   \(hash\(s\)\) is invariant throughout their lifespan. This allows strings to serve as keys in hash) Tj
T*
(   maps \(dictionaries\) and members of hash sets.) Tj
T*
(   - Memory Sharing & Interning: Identical immutable string literals can safely share the exact) Tj
T*
(   same memory address without risk of one subsystem inadvertently mutating another's data.) Tj
T*
(   - Thread Safety: Immutable strings are inherently thread-safe; concurrent threads can read) Tj
T*
(   shared strings without synchronization locks.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
endobj
