(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss the built-in type conversion and type casting functions in Python \(int, float, str, bool, complex\). Explain explicit conversion versus implicit type coercion.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Type Conversion in Python:) Tj
T*
(   Python is strongly typed: it does not permit nonsensical operations between incompatible types) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., '10' + 5 raises TypeError\). However, Python supports two categories of type conversions:) Tj
T*
(   - Implicit Type Coercion: The interpreter automatically promotes operands of lower precision to) Tj
T*
(   higher precision to prevent data loss. For example, when adding an integer and a float, the) Tj
T*
(   integer is coerced to a float: 5 + 2.5 -> 7.5.) Tj
T*
(   - Explicit Type Conversion \(Type Casting\): The developer explicitly calls built-in constructor) Tj
T*
(   functions to transform an object from one type to another.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Core Conversion Functions:) Tj
T*
(   - int\(x, base=10\): Converts numeric or string x to an integer. Can parse strings with arbitrary) Tj
T*
(   number bases \(2 to 36\). Truncates floats toward zero.) Tj
T*
(   - float\(x\): Converts string or integer to IEEE 754 64-bit floating point.) Tj
T*
(   - str\(x\): Converts any object to its human-readable string representation via __str__\(\).) Tj
T*
(   - bool\(x\): Converts object to boolean based on truthiness rules.) Tj
T*
(   - complex\(real, imag\): Constructs a complex number real + imag*1j.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

