(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Python Comments, Docstrings, and the Composition of Statements. How are docstrings accessed at runtime via __doc__ and help\(\)?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Comments in Python:) Tj
T*
(   - Single-Line Comments: Introduced with the hash character \(#\). The lexer ignores all text) Tj
T*
(   following # on that line. Used for explaining non-obvious algorithmic decisions.) Tj
T*
(   - Inline Comments: Placed on the same line as code, separated by at least two spaces \(PEP 8\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Docstrings \(Documentation Strings\):) Tj
T*
(   A docstring is a string literal appearing as the very first statement within a module,) Tj
T*
(   function, class, or method definition, demarcated by triple double quotes \(""" ... """\).) Tj
T*
(   Unlike comments, docstrings are NOT discarded by the compiler. They are compiled into bytecode) Tj
T*
(   and attached to the object's __doc__ attribute, allowing runtime introspection, automated) Tj
T*
(   documentation generation \(Sphinx, pydoc\), and IDE auto-completion.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Composition of Statements:) Tj
T*
(   Statement composition refers to combining simple statements, expressions, and control) Tj
T*
(   structures into compound expressions. Python's clean indentation-delimited block structure) Tj
T*
(   ensures high readability without bracket noise.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Python Code Demonstration:) Tj
T*
(   def calculate_cylinder_volume\(radius: float, height: float\) -> float:) Tj
T*
(   """Calculate the volume of a right circular cylinder.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Parameters:) Tj
T*
(   radius \(float\): The radial distance from center to circumference.) Tj
T*
