(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Python 2 versus Python 3 architectural differences. Focus on print, Unicode string handling, integer division, and range vs xrange.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Historical Context and Python 3 Migration:) Tj
T*
(   Python 3.0 \(released December 2008, codified in PEP 3000\) was an intentionally) Tj
T*
(   backward-incompatible major release designed to rectify fundamental design flaws in Python 2:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Core Architectural Distinctions:) Tj
T*
(   1. Print Statement vs Function:) Tj
T*
(   - Python 2: print was a language statement \(print 'Hello'\). It could not be passed as a) Tj
T*
(   callback or customized with keyword arguments.) Tj
T*
(   - Python 3: print\(\) is a built-in function \(print\('Hello', sep=' ', end='\\n',) Tj
T*
(   file=sys.stdout\)\).) Tj
T*
(   2. String and Unicode Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   - Python 2: Default str was 8-bit ASCII/bytes; unicode was a separate type \(u'text'\). Mixing) Tj
T*
(   them led to frequent UnicodeDecodeError exceptions.) Tj
T*
(   - Python 3: All str objects are Unicode \(UTF-8 decoded code points\) by default. Raw binary) Tj
T*
(   streams are encapsulated strictly in the bytes type.) Tj
T*
(   3. Integer Division:) Tj
T*
(   - Python 2: 5 / 2 evaluated to 2 \(integer truncation\).) Tj
T*
(   - Python 3: 5 / 2 evaluates to 2.5 \(true float division\). Floor division requires explicit //) Tj
T*
(   operator.) Tj
T*
(   4. Range vs XRange:) Tj
T*
(   - Python 2: range\(N\) generated an in-memory list of N integers immediately, causing high memory) Tj
T*
(   consumption for large N. xrange\(N\) was a lazy generator object.) Tj
T*
(   - Python 3: xrange was removed, and range\(\) was redesigned to behave like xrange as a lazy,) Tj
T*
(   memory-efficient sequence iterator with O\(1\) memory overhead.) Tj
T*
(   5. Long Integers:) Tj
T*
(   - Python 2 had both int \(32/64 bit\) and long \(unbounded\).) Tj
T*
(   - Python 3 unified all integers into a single unbounded int type.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
