(>> Solution to Question 30:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the if __name__ == '__main__' idiom in Python. What problem does it solve, and how does Python execute scripts versus imported modules?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Problem of Unintended Code Execution on Import:) Tj
T*
(   When Python imports a module \(e.g., import my_script\), the interpreter reads, compiles to) Tj
T*
(   bytecode, and executes every top-level statement in that file from top to bottom.) Tj
T*
(   If a script contains testing code, print statements, or server launch commands at the top) Tj
T*
(   level, those statements will execute immediately upon import by another script, causing severe) Tj
T*
(   side effects!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The __name__ Mechanism:) Tj
T*
(   The CPython interpreter automatically populates the built-in variable __name__ before executing) Tj
T*
(   a file:) Tj
T*
(   - Scenario A \(Direct Execution\): When a script is executed directly from the terminal \(python) Tj
T*
(   my_script.py\), CPython sets __name__ = '__main__'.) Tj
T*
(   - Scenario B \(Imported as a Module\): When my_script.py is imported by another module \(import) Tj
T*
(   my_script\), CPython sets __name__ = 'my_script' \(the module's actual file/package name\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
